# FPGA IP Directory Structure

## Standard IP Directory Layout

Follow this directory structure for Radiant/Propel-compatible FPGA IP blocks:

```
ip_name/
├── rtl/                    # RTL source files (Required)
│   ├── ip_name.v          # Main IP module (Verilog)
│   └── submodules/        # Internal submodules (if applicable)
│
├── testbench/              # Testbenches (Optional)
│   └── tb_top.sv          # Main testbench
│
├── doc/                    # Documentation (Required)
│   └── introduction.html  # IP documentation in HTML format
│
├── plugin/                 # Plugin scripts (Optional)
│   └── plugin.py          # Python script for metadata support functions
│
├── metadata.xml            # IP metadata definition (Required)
├── bus_interface.xml       # Bus interface definitions (Required)
├── memory_map.xml          # Memory map definitions (Required)
├── address_space.xml       # Address space definitions (Required)
└── settings.xml            # Parameter definitions (Optional)
```

**Key Points:**
- This structure is designed for Lattice Radiant and Propel integration
- XML files define IP properties for tool integration
- All XML files use Lattice-specific XML schema
- `settings.xml` can be included in `metadata.xml` or kept separate for cleaner organization

## Alternative: Multi-IP Repository Structure

For repositories containing multiple IP blocks:

```
ip_library/
├── common/                 # Shared components
│   ├── interfaces/        # Common interfaces (AXI, APB, etc.)
│   ├── packages/          # Shared packages
│   └── utils/             # Utility modules (FIFOs, sync, etc.)
│
├── uart_ip/               # UART IP
│   ├── rtl/
│   ├── tb/
│   ├── docs/
│   └── README.md
│
├── spi_ip/                # SPI IP
│   ├── rtl/
│   ├── tb/
│   ├── docs/
│   └── README.md
│
├── gpio_ip/               # GPIO IP
│   ├── rtl/
│   ├── tb/
│   ├── docs/
│   └── README.md
│
├── integration_examples/   # Multi-IP integration examples
│   └── soc_demo/
│       ├── rtl/
│       ├── tb/
│       └── README.md
│
└── scripts/               # Repository-level scripts
    ├── run_all_tests.sh
    └── generate_docs.py
```

## File Naming Conventions

### IP Module Files
- **Main module**: Match IP name exactly
  - Example: `uart_ip.sv`, `spi_master.sv`, `axi_gpio.sv`
- **Sub-modules**: Prefix with IP name
  - Example: `uart_ip_tx.sv`, `uart_ip_rx.sv`, `uart_ip_regs.sv`
- **Packages**: Suffix with `_pkg.sv`
  - Example: `uart_ip_pkg.sv`, `axi_pkg.sv`
- **Include files**: Suffix with `.svh`
  - Example: `uart_ip_defines.svh`, `config.svh`

### Testbench Files
- **Main testbench**: `<ip_name>_tb.sv`
  - Example: `uart_ip_tb.sv`
- **Test cases**: Prefix with `test_`
  - Example: `test_basic.sv`, `test_performance.sv`
- **Models**: Descriptive names with `_model` suffix
  - Example: `memory_model.sv`, `bus_monitor.sv`

### Documentation Files
- **HTML Documentation**: `doc/introduction.html` (Required for Radiant/Propel)
  - Contains IP overview, supported devices, and references
  - Uses standard HTML format

### XML Configuration Files

#### metadata.xml (Required)
The main IP descriptor file that defines the IP's identity and integration properties.

**Key Sections:**
- **`<lsccip:general>`**: IP identification
  - `vendor`: IP vendor (e.g., "latticesemi.com")
  - `name`: IP module name
  - `display_name`: Human-readable name
  - `version`: Semantic version (e.g., "1.0.0")
  - `category`: IP category for tool organization
  - `keywords`: Searchable keywords (e.g., "BusType_AXI")
  - `supported_products`: Target FPGA families and devices

- **`<lsccip:settings>`**: Parameter definitions
  - Can be defined inline or via `<xi:include href="settings.xml"/>`
  - Each setting defines: id, type, value_type, default, options, etc.

- **`<lsccip:ports>`**: Port definitions
  - Maps physical ports to bus interfaces
  - Defines port direction, width, and type (clock, reset, etc.)

- **XML Includes**: References to external XML files
  ```xml
  <xi:include parse="xml" href="bus_interface.xml" />
  <xi:include parse="xml" href="address_space.xml" />
  <xi:include parse="xml" href="memory_map.xml" />
  ```

#### bus_interface.xml (Required)
Defines all bus interfaces exposed by the IP.

**Structure:**
- **`<lsccip:busInterface>`**: One for each interface
  - `name`: Interface instance name (e.g., "AXI_DATA_S00")
  - `displayName`: Display name for tools
  - `description`: Human-readable description
  - `busType`: Standard bus protocol (e.g., AXI4, AXI4-Lite, custom)
  - `portMaps`: Maps logical protocol signals to physical port names
  - `slave` or `master`: Interface direction with memory map reference

**Common Bus Types:**
- **AXI4**: Full AXI4 protocol for data transfers
- **AXI4-Lite**: Simplified AXI for register access
- **Interrupt**: Interrupt signal interface
- **Custom**: Vendor-specific protocols (e.g., HyperBus)

#### memory_map.xml (Required)
Defines memory-mapped address spaces for slave interfaces.

**Structure:**
- **`<lsccip:memoryMap>`**: One for each addressable interface
  - `name`: Memory map identifier
  - `description`: Purpose description
  - `addressBlock`: Address block definition
    - `baseAddress`: Starting address (typically 0 for relative addressing)
    - `range`: Size in bytes
    - `width`: Data width in bits

**Example Use Cases:**
- Control register space (typically 4KB for AXI-Lite)
- Data memory space (e.g., 16MB for memory controller access)

#### address_space.xml (Required)
Defines address spaces for master interfaces.

**Structure:**
- **`<lsccip:addressSpace>`**: One for each master interface
  - `name`: Address space identifier
  - `range`: Total addressable range (in hexadecimal)
  - `width`: Address bus width in bits

**Common Ranges:**
- `0x100000000`: 4GB address space (32-bit addressing)
- Custom ranges for specific memory controllers

#### settings.xml (Optional)
Separates parameter definitions from metadata.xml for cleaner organization.

**Structure:**
- **`<lsccip:settings>`**: Contains all `<lsccip:setting>` elements

**Setting Attributes:**
- `id`: Parameter name (matches HDL parameter)
- `type`: "param" for module parameters
- `value_type`: "int", "bool", "string", etc.
- `conn_mod`: Target module name
- `title`: Display name in GUI
- `default`: Default value
- `options`: List of valid values (e.g., "[1, 2]") or range (e.g., "range(1,33)")
- `value_range`: Min/max bounds (e.g., "(0, 2)")
- `editable`: Boolean or conditional expression
- `group1`, `group2`: GUI organization grouping

**When to Use:**
- Use inline in metadata.xml for small IPs (<10 parameters)
- Use separate settings.xml for complex IPs with many parameters
- Improves readability and maintainability

## IP Organization Best Practices

### 1. Self-Contained IP
- Each IP should be independently compilable
- Minimize external dependencies
- Clearly document any required dependencies
- Include all necessary files in the IP directory

### 2. Parameterization
- Use parameters/generics for configurability
- Document all parameters in the datasheet
- Provide sensible defaults
- Example:
  ```systemverilog
  module uart_ip #(
      parameter ADDR_WIDTH = 16,
      parameter DATA_WIDTH = 32,
      parameter CLK_FREQ   = 100_000_000,
      parameter BAUD_RATE  = 115200
  ) (...);
  ```

### 3. Interface Standards
- Use industry-standard interfaces (AXI, APB, Avalon, Wishbone)
- Document interface protocol clearly
- Provide interface compatibility information
- Include timing diagrams

### 4. Register Interface
- Follow consistent register addressing scheme
- Provide register definitions in multiple formats:
  - Markdown tables
  - C header files (.h)
  - SystemVerilog packages
  - SystemRDL (optional)

## IP Versioning

### Version Numbering
Use semantic versioning: `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`

- **MAJOR**: Incompatible API/interface changes
- **MINOR**: Backwards-compatible functionality additions
- **PATCH**: Backwards-compatible bug fixes

### Version Documentation
- Maintain `CHANGELOG.md` in IP root
- Tag releases in version control
- Document interface changes clearly
- Provide migration guides for major version changes

## Build Artifacts & Version Control

### Git-Ignored Files
Add to `.gitignore`:
```
# Simulation artifacts
sim/work/
sim/*.wlf
sim/*.log
*.vcd
*.fst

# Synthesis artifacts
syn/output/
syn/reports/
*.rpt


# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
```

### Files to Commit
**Always commit:**
- All RTL source files (`rtl/`)
- Testbenches (`tb/`)
- Documentation (`docs/`)
- Simulation scripts (`sim/*.tcl`, `sim/Makefile`)
- Synthesis constraints (`syn/*.sdc`)
- README and licensing info
- CHANGELOG.md

**Never commit:**
- Compiled simulation executables
- Waveform files
- Log files
- Tool-generated reports
- Vendor project files (unless small team sharing exact setup)

### Plugin Directory (Optional)

The `plugin/` directory contains Python scripts that provide supporting functions for the IP integration.

**Structure:**
```
plugin/
├── plugin.py          # Main plugin script
└── data/              # Plugin data files (if needed)
    └── pkgExtData.xml
```

**Purpose of plugin.py:**
- Provides callback functions for Propel/Radiant integration
- Performs dynamic parameter validation
- Calculates derived values based on user settings
- Generates or modifies metadata dynamically
- Implements custom GUI behavior

**Common Use Cases:**
- Validating parameter combinations
- Calculating timing constraints based on clock frequencies
- Generating file lists for synthesis
- Creating custom configuration outputs

## IP Documentation Requirements

### 1. doc/introduction.html (Required)
HTML-formatted documentation for Radiant/Propel display.

**Must include:**
- IP name and description
- Supported devices (FPGA families)
- Key features
- References (links to user guides, datasheets)
- Revision history

**Example Structure:**
```html
<HEAD>
  <TITLE>IP Name</TITLE>
</HEAD>
<BODY>
  <H1>IP Name</H1>
  <H2>Description</H2>
  <P>IP description...</P>

  <H2>Devices Supported</H2>
  <P>Device list...</P>

  <H2>References</H2>
  <UL>
    <LI><A HREF="url">User Guide</A></LI>
  </UL>

  <H2>Revision History</H2>
  <TABLE>
    <TR><TD><B>Version</B></TD><TD><B>Changes</B></TD></TR>
    <TR><TD>1.0.0</TD><TD>Initial release</TD></TR>
  </TABLE>
</BODY>
```

### 2. XML Documentation
All documentation is embedded within XML files:
- **metadata.xml**: IP identity, version, supported devices
- **bus_interface.xml**: Interface descriptions
- **memory_map.xml**: Address space descriptions
- **Port descriptions**: Within metadata.xml port definitions

### 3. External Documentation (Optional)
For detailed documentation, maintain separate files:
- User guide (PDF)
- Application notes
- Example designs
- Reference these in `doc/introduction.html`

## IP Creation Checklist

When creating a new Radiant/Propel-compatible IP:

- [ ] Create directory structure (rtl/, doc/, optionally testbench/, plugin/)
- [ ] Implement RTL with proper parameterization
- [ ] Add comprehensive header comments to RTL files
- [ ] Create testbench (tb_top.sv) if needed
- [ ] Create metadata.xml with IP identification and port definitions
- [ ] Create bus_interface.xml with all interface definitions
- [ ] Create memory_map.xml for slave interfaces
- [ ] Create address_space.xml for master interfaces
- [ ] Create settings.xml or embed settings in metadata.xml
- [ ] Create doc/introduction.html with IP documentation
- [ ] Create plugin/plugin.py if custom logic needed (optional)
- [ ] Validate all XML files against Lattice schema
- [ ] Test IP integration in Propel
- [ ] Run lint checks on RTL
- [ ] Verify testbench passes (if applicable)
- [ ] Tag initial version (v1.0.0)

## Linting & Code Quality

### Linting Tools
Place configurations in the IP root directory:
- **Verilator**: `.verilator_config`
- **Verible**: `.verible.lint`
- **Synopsys Spyglass**: `spyglass.prj`

### Coding Standards for Lattice IP
- Use consistent indentation (2 or 4 spaces)
- One module per file for main IP module
- Use Verilog (.v) or SystemVerilog (.sv) as appropriate
- Meaningful signal/port names
- Comprehensive comments for complex logic
- Parameters for all configurable values (match metadata.xml settings)
- Avoid hardcoded magic numbers
- Clock domain crossing (CDC) annotations
- Active-low resets named with `_n` suffix
- Assert statements for critical assumptions

Example header comment:
```verilog
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module: hyperram_mc
// Description: HyperRAM Memory Controller with AXI4 interface
// Version: 1.0.0
// Company: Lattice Semiconductor
//
// Parameters:
//   HYPERRAM_NUM - Number of HyperRAM devices
//   ID_W         - AXI ID width
//   SYSBUS_CLOCK_MHZ - System clock frequency in MHz
//
// Ports:
//   clk_i              - System clock
//   rst_n              - Active-low reset
//   hyperbus_clk_i     - HyperBus clock
//   s_axi_*            - AXI4 data interface
//   s_axil_*           - AXI4-Lite control interface
//-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
```

### Parameter Naming Convention
Parameters in RTL **must match** settings defined in metadata.xml or settings.xml:

```verilog
// RTL
module ip_name #(
    parameter HYPERRAM_NUM = 2,
    parameter ID_W = 1,
    parameter SYSBUS_CLOCK_MHZ = 100
) (...);
```

```xml
<!-- metadata.xml or settings.xml -->
<lsccip:setting id="HYPERRAM_NUM" type="param" ... />
<lsccip:setting id="ID_W" type="param" ... />
<lsccip:setting id="SYSBUS_CLOCK_MHZ" type="param" ... />
```



## Testing Strategy for IP

### Testbench Structure
For Radiant/Propel IPs, testbenches are optional but recommended for verification.

**Location:** `testbench/tb_top.sv`

**Testbench Requirements:**
- Self-contained verification environment
- Tests all major functional paths
- Verifies bus interface compliance (AXI, AXI-Lite, etc.)
- Includes test scenarios for:
  - Basic functionality
  - Edge cases and corner cases
  - Error conditions
  - Performance/timing verification

### Simulation Approach
1. **Unit Tests**: Test IP in isolation using tb_top.sv
2. **Interface Tests**: Verify bus protocol compliance
3. **Integration Tests**: Test within Propel-generated SoC
4. **Hardware Validation**: Test on target FPGA board

### Example Testbench Structure
```systemverilog
// testbench/tb_top.sv
module tb_top;
    // Clock and reset generation
    reg clk_i, rst_n;

    // Bus functional models
    // - AXI4 master model
    // - AXI4-Lite master model

    // DUT instantiation
    ip_name #(...) dut (...);

    // Test scenarios
    initial begin
        // Test 1: Basic functionality
        // Test 2: Bus protocol compliance
        // Test 3: Error conditions
        // Test 4: Performance verification
        $finish;
    end
endmodule
```

## Release Process

### Creating a Release
1. Update version number in `metadata.xml`:
   ```xml
   <lsccip:version>1.2.0</lsccip:version>
   ```
2. Update revision history in `doc/introduction.html`
3. Update RTL header comments with new version
4. Run lint checks on all RTL
5. Verify testbench passes (if applicable)
6. Test IP integration in Propel
7. Validate all XML files
8. Create git tag: `git tag -a v1.2.0 -m "Release v1.2.0"`
9. Push tag: `git push origin v1.2.0`
10. Archive IP package for distribution

### Version Numbering in XML
All version information centralized in metadata.xml:
```xml
<lsccip:general>
    <lsccip:version>1.2.0</lsccip:version>
    <lsccip:min_radiant_version>3.1</lsccip:min_radiant_version>
</lsccip:general>
```

### Revision History in HTML
Update `doc/introduction.html`:
```html
<H2>Revision History</H2>
<TABLE cellpadding="10">
  <TR>
    <TD><B>1.2.0</B></TD>
    <TD>Added new feature X. Fixed bug Y.</TD>
  </TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><B>1.1.0</B></TD>
    <TD>Improved performance.</TD>
  </TR>
  <TR>
    <TD><B>1.0.0</B></TD>
    <TD>Initial release.</TD>
  </TR>
</TABLE>
```

## License Considerations

For Lattice IPs, licensing is typically:
- **Lattice Reference Design License**: For Lattice-provided IPs
- **Proprietary/Commercial**: For commercial third-party IPs
- **Open Source**: If distributing publicly (MIT, Apache, BSD, etc.)

**License Information:**
- Add copyright headers to all source files
- Include license text in `doc/introduction.html`
- Specify licensing in metadata.xml if applicable

**Example RTL Header:**
```verilog
//=============================================================================
// Copyright (c) 2024 [Your Company/Lattice Semiconductor]
// Licensed under [License Name]
//=============================================================================
```
